Let's Understand the Complete Flow:
===================================

Suppose PC0 wants to ping PC1
    1) PC0 checks:
        Destination = 192.168.20.20
        My Network = 192.168.10.0/24

    2) different network:
        I will send it to my gateway

    3) PC0 sends the frame to:
        Destination MAC = Router's MAC
        Destination IP = 192.168.20.20
        Layer2(MAC): router
        Layer3(IP): Final destination (PC1)

    4) The switch receives the frame,
       it knows the frame came from VLAN 10
       before sending it to the router over the trunk, it adds:
       802.1Q Tag
       VLAN ID = 10

    5) The router receives the frame:
        it reads the VLAN tag
        VLAN ID = 10
        The router says: This belongs to subinterface G0/0.10
        The frame enters GigabitEthernet0/0.10.

    6) The router examines the IP destination:
        Destination IP = 192.168.20.20
        It knows  192.168.20.0/24
        G0/0.20
        So it routes the packet internally from G0/0.10 to G0/0.20

    7) Before sending the frame back to the switch, the router tags it:
        VLAN ID = 20
       the switch receives it on the trunk, removes VLAN tag, and forwards it only the port
       belonging to VLAN 20 (fa0/2), where PC1 receives it

    8) Why do we use subinterfaces:
         - to allow one physical router interface to serve multiple VLANs,
         - each subinterface acts as the default gateway for a different VLAN


    9) What does encapsulation dot1Q 10 do:
        - it associates the subinterface with VLAN 10
        - the router processes all frames tagged with VLAN ID 10 that subinterface
